# ── Telegram ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bot token from @BotFather
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
# Target channel id. Either spelling works — the bare id Telegram's UI shows
# (2321540352) or the full Bot API form (-1002321540352); core/api.ts
# normalises to the full form. Note TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID below is NOT normalised
# and must be the full -100… id.
TELEGRAM_CHANNEL_ID=
# Public link of the channel (https://t.me/yourchannel)
TELEGRAM_CHANNEL_PUBLIC_LINK=
# Discussion group id (the comments thread attached to the channel)
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# ── VK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VK access token with group/wall permissions
VK_TOKEN=

# ── VK user token for wall photos (the route that works) ──────────────────
# Attaching a photo to a wall post needs a USER token holding `photos`+`wall`.
# A community token cannot (Code 27) and a VK ID app is not granted those
# rights (Code 15) — see src/lib/vkuser.ts. A VK MINI APP is, and Mini Apps are
# still created outside the VK ID business cabinet.
#
# How to get one:
#   1. Create a Mini App at https://vk.ru/editapp?act=create
#   2. Point its URL at a page that calls VK Bridge:
#        bridge.send("VKWebAppGetAuthToken", {app_id, scope: "photos,wall"})
#      (frontend/public/vk-probe.html in the cyberjab repo does exactly this)
#   4. Check it from this host any time with `npm run vkuserprobe`.
#   3. Open it inside VK as a community ADMIN, approve, copy the access_token.
#
# Caveats, both real:
#   • The token is IP-BOUND. Code 5 "given to another ip address" means the
#     host's egress IP no longer matches — get a fresh token from that network.
#     On pxlserv-ubuntu this needs a firewall exception. The nft table
#     `dockervpn` marks opted-in containers (porter2 among them) with 0x1, and
#     `ip rule` routes marked packets to the VPN gateway 10.10.3.1, whose exit
#     IP rotates in a NL pool — so every VK call failed Code 5 until VK was
#     excluded from the mark. In /etc/dockervpn.nft, ABOVE the mark rule:
#       ip daddr 87.240.128.0/18 return   # api.vk.ru/.com + pu.vk.com uploads
#       ip daddr 93.186.224.0/20 return   # rest of the API pool
#       ip daddr 95.213.0.0/18   return   # oauth./login.vk.ru — token renewal
#     The third prefix is easy to miss and fails INTERMITTENTLY without it:
#     oauth.vk.ru resolves to BOTH 93.186.237.1 (covered by rule two) and
#     95.213.56.1 (covered by nothing), so roughly half of all connections
#     left via the VPN and reached VK from a Dutch exit. Same split applies to
#     login.vk.com, so a redirect chain could change country mid-flow.
#     Destination-scoped on purpose — a source match would pin a container IP
#     that docker-vpn-selector re-resolves on every restart.
#     Unmarked traffic leaves via 192.168.1.1 = 91.238.229.242, the IP the
#     token is bound to. Telegram is untouched: 91.108.x, 149.154.160.0/20 and
#     185.76.151.0/24 match neither prefix, so it still takes the VPN.
#     Correct end state, verified with `npm run vkuserprobe` in the container:
#     `egress:` shows a 185.229.191.x NL address AND checks [1]-[3] pass.
#     GOTCHA: re-running /usr/local/sbin/dockervpn-nft-init.sh empties the
#     vpn_hosts set, and the selector caches its last computed state so it will
#     NOT repopulate — always follow it with
#     `systemctl restart docker-vpn-selector`, or every container silently
#     drops off the VPN and Telegram starts timing out.
#   • It EXPIRES AFTER 24 HOURS. Measured, not guessed: the OAuth flow reports
#     expires_in=86400. There is no refresh token and no permanent variant —
#     the `offline` scope that used to grant one has been removed, and
#     oauth.vk.* now answers 401 "invalid scope" for it in any combination
#     while accepting a nonsense scope with 200.
#     So the failure would be a daily certainty — except the token now RENEWS
#     ITSELF (see the automatic-renewal block under this variable). porter-
#     config.ts → vkToken.watch stays on as the safety net: it renews ahead of
#     the deadline and DMs SUPER_ADMIN_ID only when automation could not save it.
#
# THIS VARIABLE IS A BOOTSTRAP VALUE, NOT THE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
# The live token lives in db/vkuser.json (0600, gitignored) so a replacement
# takes effect without an edit and a restart. Precedence, in one line: the
# store wins, UNLESS this variable has changed since the store was written, in
# which case your edit wins. Nothing here can be silently ignored in either
# direction. Override the store's path with VK_USER_TOKEN_STORE.
#
# Treat it like a password: it acts as the admin who approved it.
VK_USER_TOKEN=

# ── Automatic renewal of the user token ───────────────────────────────────
# The 24 h expiry needs no human anymore. When fewer than renewAheadHours
# (porter.config.ts, default 18) remain, the watchdog exchanges the held token
# for a fresh one via POST login.vk.ru/?act=web_token — the same exchange VK's
# own web client makes for an approved Mini App. It authenticates with your
# BROWSER SESSION (the request body carries no token at all), not with any
# password or consent step, which is why it can run unattended where the OAuth
# chain refused everything.
#
# That session lives in a cookie jar you export ONCE per login epoch:
#   db/vkcookies.txt   (override with VK_COOKIE_FILE; gitignored, chmod 0600)
# Any ONE of these three formats works:
#   • Cookie header      — DevTools → Network → a vk.ru request → copy the
#                          whole `Cookie:` request-header line into the file.
#   • Netscape cookies.txt — what curl / yt-dlp / "Get cookies.txt" produce;
#                            paste unchanged.
#   • Cookie-Editor JSON   — an array of {name, value} objects; paste unchanged.
#   • DevTools cookies table — Application tab → Cookies → select the rows →
#                            copy; select the WHOLE list, not just the view.
# Export the WHOLE jar from the SAME tab that talks to vk.ru. `remixsid` (the
# classic session) must be there, plus at least ONE token-carrier cookie — the
# web session keeps its own API token in `p`/`sua` (older sessions) or in
# `remixnsid`/`remixnttpid` (newer exports); absence produces
# {"error_info":"unauthorized"} that no body tweak fixes. An export method
# that cannot see HttpOnly cookies is the wrong method. As a bonus, those
# carrier cookies themselves hold vk1.a tokens — if the exchange ever
# refuses, renewal falls back to validating one of those directly.
#
# ⚠ THE JAR IS ACCOUNT-GRADE — whoever holds it IS the account. It never gets
# logged, and like the token it is gitignored and 0600.
#
# The jar goes stale on its own schedule (logout elsewhere, password change,
# VK security review). Renewal then reports why in `[vk-renew]`/`[vk-watch]`
# log lines and the watchdog alerts SUPER_ADMIN_ID with that reason; re-export
# a fresh jar from a logged-in vk.ru tab and recovery happens within the hour.
# Test the route end-to-end (dry run by default):
#   docker compose exec porter2 npm run vkrenewprobe             # report only
#   docker compose exec porter2 npm run vkrenewprobe -- --install
#     ↑ also installs the validated token — the manual rotation path
#
# Optional: app id sent in the exchange body. Defaults to OUR Mini App with a
# fallback to the id VK's web client uses for itself; every minted token is
# validated against photos.getWallUploadServer before it replaces anything,
# so a wrong-scope answer is discarded rather than installed.
VK_COOKIE_FILE=
VK_APP_ID=

# ── Submissions / moderation (frontend-support module) ────────────────────
# Telegram chat that receives moderation messages (include the -100 prefix)
MODERATION_CHAT_ID=
# Forum topic id within the moderation chat (omit if the chat is not a forum)
MODERATION_TOPIC_ID=
# VK group id to publish approved submissions to — MUST be negative (e.g. -228995635)
VK_GROUP_ID=

# ── API server (frontend-support module) ──────────────────────────────────
# Value sent by clients in the x-api-key header for /api/* routes
PORTER_API_KEY=
# Express listen port (default 5050)
PORTER_API_PORT=5050
# Public base URL used to resolve uploaded image URLs (must be reachable by Telegram)
PORTER_PUBLIC_URL=
# Public HTTPS URL of the Next.js Mini App — used as fallback for the moderation
# button when TMA_APP_NAME is not set. Example: https://frog.dev.lok1s.tech
TMA_PUBLIC_URL=
# Comma-separated Telegram user ids allowed to moderate. The moderation deep
# link travels in a group message and can be forwarded, so a valid Telegram
# identity is not enough on its own — this is the list that decides.
# Leave blank to use the administrators of MODERATION_CHAT_ID instead, which
# needs no upkeep. SUPER_ADMIN_ID is always allowed either way.
MODERATOR_IDS=

# Short name of the Web App registered in BotFather via /newapp (e.g. "moderate").
# MUST be 3-30 chars of a-z, A-Z, 0-9 or _ — Telegram rejects anything else, and
# a display-style name here yields a dead link with no init data.
# When set, the moderation button uses a t.me/<bot>/<app>?startapp=sub_<id> deep
# link, which opens the Mini App with full Telegram Web App SDK context from any
# chat type (group, channel, private). Preferred over the plain URL fallback.
TMA_APP_NAME=

# ── MTProto (optional, framework capability) ──────────────────────────────
# A second authorization used for what the Bot API cannot do — currently the
# full chat member list behind @all. Leave blank to disable; every consumer
# falls back to Bot-API-only behaviour.
#
# api_id / api_hash come from https://my.telegram.org/apps
MTPROTO_API_ID=
MTPROTO_API_HASH=
# User-account session string from `npm run mtlogin`. Optional — without it the
# bot token is used instead, which requires the bot to be an ADMINISTRATOR of
# every chat it is asked to enumerate. Treat this string like a password.
MTPROTO_SESSION=
# Where the auth key and peer cache are kept (default ./db/mtproto.session)
MTPROTO_SESSION_FILE=

# ── /health command (porter.config.ts → health) ───────────────────────────
# OFF by default. Turn it on in porter.config.ts, not here — it reports on live
# credentials and infrastructure, so exposing it should be a deliberate choice.
# Access uses the ids below and needs no extra variables:
#   SUPER_ADMIN_ID  → detailed report (adds uptime, memory, ids, sizes)
#   MODERATOR_IDS   → brief report (one verdict per service)
#   chat admins     → brief report, in that chat
# Everyone else is refused. No token, key or session string is ever rendered,
# in either form.

# ── @all module ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Telegram user id that always passes admin checks (fallback/owner)
SUPER_ADMIN_ID=
# Optional symbol to replace default member tags (e.g. ".", "|", "•")
ALL_TAG_SYMBOL=

